    "locked_model": "Sum of split (asymmetric) pseudo-Voigt profiles, one per Bragg reflection, on a SNIP background (snip_iterations='auto'). eta (Gaussian-Lorentzian mixing) free per peak in [0,1]. Expected ~7\u20139 significant reflections: ~11.0\u00b0, ~17.7\u00b0, ~18.6\u00b0, ~19.2\u00b0 (weak), ~22.2\u00b0 (strong), ~24.6\u00b0 (weak/borderline), ~26.0\u00b0 (broad), ~29.5\u00b0, plus weaker high-angle features. Peaks within 1.5\u00d7FWHM (the 17.7/18.6/19.2 cluster) are fit jointly within the single global model.",
    "is_single_spectrum": false
  },
  "detailed_analysis": "Two in-situ synchrotron XRD patterns during a dehydration ramp were fit with a global split-pseudo-Voigt-per-reflection model on a SNIP background, achieving global R\u00b2\u22481.0000 and peak-region R\u00b2\u22480.999 at both temperatures \u2014 an excellent, complete fit with no unmodelled-reflection residual and no flags or refits. From 33.4 to 35.9 \u00b0C the dominant reflections shift coherently to lower 2\u03b8 by ~0.004\u20130.008\u00b0 (higher-angle peaks shifting more in absolute 2\u03b8), the direction and angle-dependence expected for a real lattice expansion, which for a variable-temperature run is a legitimately trackable structural change rather than a suspect displacement artifact per the domain sample-and-mounting priors. All dominant integrated areas rise. These trends are directionally coherent but rest on only two temperature points separated by 2.5 \u00b0C, so no slope is statistically validated and the intensity rise cannot be cleanly separated from incident-flux/normalization drift. The size/strain analysis is unreliable and should not be over-interpreted: per-peak Scherrer sizes span >3\u00d7 (~15\u201344 nm), triggering the anisotropic-broadening flag; the Scherrer mean (~27 nm) disagrees strongly with the W-H size (~46 nm) at 33.4 \u00b0C; and W-H size and microstrain drop together, the signature of an ill-conditioned two-point W-H rather than genuine strain relaxation. Additionally, spectrum 0's \u03b7 values pinned at the [0,1] bounds mean its per-peak profile shape (and thus FWHM-derived sizes) is poorly constrained. The robust, defensible finding is the coherent small shift to lower 2\u03b8 consistent with lattice expansion during the dehydration step; the crystallite-size/strain story is not supported at the required confidence.",
  "scientific_claims": [
    {
      "claim": "During the 33.4\u219235.9 \u00b0C step of the in-situ dehydration ramp, all dominant Bragg reflections shift coherently to lower 2\u03b8 (~0.004\u20130.008\u00b0), consistent with a small lattice expansion, accompanied by a uniform increase in integrated peak areas.",
      "scientific_impact": "Tracking the sign and magnitude of the cell response across a dehydration ramp constrains the framework's thermal/dehydration behavior and distinguishes reversible expansion from dehydration-driven structural change; even a small coherent shift is the primary observable of interest in an in-situ run.",
      "has_anyone_question": "Has anyone quantified the unit-cell expansion and intensity evolution of this material across a finely sampled dehydration temperature ramp to establish whether the response is continuous thermal expansion or a dehydration-linked structural transition?",
      "keywords": [
        "in-situ XRD",
        "dehydration",
        "lattice expansion",
        "peak shift",
        "synchrotron"
      ]
    }
  ],
